{% doc %}
  One row of a footer link column: the link, and the hours that belong to it
  if any have been given for that name.

  The hours are matched here rather than stored with the link because the
  links come from a Shopify navigation menu, which has nowhere to keep them.
  Matching reduces both sides to letters, so the two lists can each be
  written the way they read best.

  @param {string} title - The link's label
  @param {string} url - Where it goes
  @param {string[]} [hour_lines] - Lines of "Name | Hours", already split
{% enddoc %}

{% liquid
  assign label = title | strip

  # Both sides are reduced to letters before comparing, so "OCP Crows Nest",
  # "Crows Nest" and "crows-nest" are one name. The menu is written for the
  # reader and the hours for whoever maintains them; neither should have to
  # match the other keystroke for keystroke.
  assign key = label | downcase | remove: 'ocp' | remove: ' ' | remove: '-' | remove: "'"

  # The link's page handle, as a second thing to match on: a menu reading
  # "Crows Nest" and a page at /pages/crows-nest reduce to the same letters
  # even when the two lists word the name differently.
  assign handle_key = url | split: '?' | first | split: '/' | last | downcase | remove: 'ocp' | remove: '-' | remove: '_'

  assign hours = ''
  for line in hour_lines
    assign pair = line | split: '|'
    if pair[1] == blank
      continue
    endif
    assign candidate = pair[0] | downcase | remove: 'ocp' | remove: ' ' | remove: '-' | remove: "'"
    if candidate == key or candidate == handle_key
      assign hours = pair[1] | strip
      break
    endif
  endfor

  # Nothing matched outright. Try again allowing one name to sit inside the
  # other, so "George St" finds "George Street" and "Crows Nest" finds "Crows
  # Nest Shop". Held to names of four letters or more, since a short one
  # would start matching things it has no business matching.
  if hours == blank and key.size > 3
    for line in hour_lines
      assign pair = line | split: '|'
      if pair[1] == blank
        continue
      endif
      assign candidate = pair[0] | downcase | remove: 'ocp' | remove: ' ' | remove: '-' | remove: "'"
      if candidate.size > 3
        if candidate contains key or key contains candidate
          assign hours = pair[1] | strip
          break
        endif
      endif
    endfor
  endif
%}

{%- comment -%}
  Both cells are always written, even when there are no hours for this name.
  The row is `display: contents`, so its children are the grid's items — and
  one row contributing a single cell shifts every cell after it, which turns
  one unmatched shop into a column of names and times that no longer line up.
{%- endcomment -%}
<li class="{% if hour_lines != blank %}footer-column__pair{% endif %}">
  <a
    class="footer-column__link"
    href="{{ url | strip }}"
  >
    {{- label -}}
  </a>

  {% if hour_lines != blank %}
    <span class="footer-column__hours">{{ hours }}</span>
  {% endif %}
</li>
